preConditionHook:
// THE GLOSSARY GAP REVERSAL: "the current bar extends one tick beyond the prior
// bar back into the gap". The PRIOR bar was a session opening gap bar -- the
// first bar of a session, opening beyond the previous session's extreme -- and
// THIS bar has traded at least reversalTicks ticks back through that bar's near
// extreme, into the gap. After a gap UP that is a fade SHORT below the gap
// bar's low; after a gap DOWN it is the long above its high. The merged
// pseudocode spelled the gap-up case, which is the SELL setup; the pair is
// mirrored, so the long side is written first here.
//
// SessionOpeningGapBeyondPriorExtreme IS INLINED, not referenced -- rules in
// this corpus never reference each other -- and carries its comment's
// substance: a session starts when the wall-clock close date changes
// (day_of(ctx.Time(0)) > day_of(ctx.Time(1)), the twin's date > date[1]);
// prevHigh / prevLow ARE HighD(1) / LowD(1), VERIFIED to slice on that same
// calendar date, to be INCLUSIVE of the current bar at offset 0 and to return a
// -1 SENTINEL while fewer than that many complete days exist, which is guarded
// on both sides because -1 as a price sits under every real price. sawStart
// makes the warm-up honest: a day is published as "the prior session" only once
// its own first bar has been observed, so the mirror is silent through the
// first partial day and the first fully observed day, and the twins agree
// exactly from the second date change on. The mirror errs toward no signal.
//
// THE ONE-BAR LAG. The EL twin reads gapUp[1] directly, because an EasyLanguage
// VARIABLE carries its own history; a C++ local is a class member with no
// history, so the previous bar's value is carried forward explicitly at the top
// of the hook before it is overwritten. The hook runs every bar in both twins,
// so the two are the same sequence. Both prev flags start false, as EL's
// gapUp[1] reads false before any history exists.
//
// Not SessionGapFirstBarReversalEntry, which needs the session's FIRST bar to
// close as a reversal bar and the SECOND to close back through its high -- a
// close-based two-bar test on a body fraction. This is the glossary's bare
// one-tick poke back into the gap, with no body test at all. Intraday bars
// only: on a daily series every bar is its own session.
// THE TICK is EL's own MinMove / PriceScale, both VERIFIED and per-symbol
// constants for the whole run, written DIVISION-FIRST in both twins so the C++
// reads token for token against its EasyLanguage. Read once into a local.
//
// Scratch below is declared in localVariables, not at hook scope: the generator
// only prefixes declared locals, so a hook-scope declaration is a redefinition
// when this rule is placed twice in one strategy. Every one is fully assigned
// each bar before it is read.
tickSz = ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale();
gapUpPrev = gapUp;
gapDnPrev = gapDn;
if (day_of(ctx.Time(0)) > day_of(ctx.Time(1))) {
    if (sawStart) {
        prevHigh = curHigh;
        prevLow = curLow;
    }
    sawStart = true;
    curHigh = high[0];
    curLow = low[0];
    firstBar = true;
} else {
    if (el_gt(high[0], curHigh)) curHigh = high[0];
    if (el_lt(low[0], curLow)) curLow = low[0];
    firstBar = false;
}
gapUp = firstBar && !el_eq(prevHigh, -1.0) && el_gt(open[0], prevHigh);
gapDn = firstBar && !el_eq(prevLow, -1.0) && el_lt(open[0], prevLow);
